+++ b/bsd/dev/pandora_cell.c
@@ -0,0 +1,2730 @@
+/*
+ * PANDORA CELL: en1, the fake Ethernet interface the modem's IP packets ride on, the DHCP and ARP
+ * that make iOS use it, and the QMI sessions that keep it up.
+ *
+ * This is HakiOS's bsd/dev/hakios.cell.c, its bsd/dev/hakios.nas.c, and the WDS / WDA / DPM / UIM /
+ * DMS / NAS half of drivers/modem/hakios.mpss.c, ported to this tree (SM8850 "canoe", X85 modem,
+ * IPA 5.5, iOS 16). The wire format and the transaction table are in bsd/dev/pandora_qmi.c; this
+ * file is everything above them. The interface is HakiOS's hakios.net.c shim, which this tree
+ * already has an equivalent of in bsd/dev/qcom/PeachNet.c -- that file's comment at :285-287 says
+ * so in as many words ("See HackiOS redwood_net.c's BSD interface integration"), and its attach
+ * sequence, its BPF taps and its link event are what 10.2.1 below copies.
+ *
+ * Where every part came from (work/baseband-port/cell-nas-qmi-spec.md):
+ *
+ *   1.2   the MAC addresses, the forced /24, the made-up router, the MTU that is DHCP's only
+ *   1.3   the downlink: an IP packet from the IPA becomes a frame from the router to us
+ *   1.4   the uplink: ARP first, then DHCP, then everything else is an IP packet to the modem;
+ *         IPv6 and the rest are counted and not carried
+ *   1.6   the interface: ifnet_allocate_extended / ifnet_attach / bpfattach, the callback set
+ *   1.7   en1's unit number is given, not allocated (IFNET_INIT_LEGACY)
+ *   2     DHCP: this machine is the DHCP server; the modem only supplies static settings
+ *   3     WDS: bind mux, set IP family, start the network interface, get current settings
+ *   4     WDA: set data format (raw IP, QMAP both ways, the embedded endpoint)
+ *   5     DPM: open the hardware data port, with this IPA's endpoint pair
+ *   7     UIM: get card status, switch slot, change provisioning session
+ *   8     DMS (online) and NAS (register, serving system, signal)
+ *   9.1   why no baseband node may ever be added -- THE WARNING AT THE TOP OF pandora_cell.h
+ *   10.2  the function list, in that order, which is the order of the sections below
+ *   10.6  the boot arguments
+ *
+ * THE ONE RULE THAT MUST NOT BE BROKEN (spec 9.1): do NOT add a "baseband" (or "bbdev") node to
+ * the device tree, and do not publish or match an AppleBaseband / AppleIPAppender service for this
+ * port. mkadt_pandora.py has none today (its comments at :245 and :250 say so) and that must stay
+ * true: a baseband node makes CommCenter take its "this device has a baseband" path, fault in
+ * ApplePDPHelperInterface::callStructMethod on a NULL std::mutex, and take SIGSEGV/SIGABRT every
+ * five to ten seconds. The data path is this fake Ethernet interface and nothing else: en1 is not
+ * pdp_ip0, it is not in the Cellular Data switch, and it carries no signal bars.
+ *
+ * Two hazards the spec flags, both of which this file handles and neither of which HakiOS writes
+ * down:
+ *
+ *   1. ifnet_event() with KEV_DL_LINK_ON / KEV_DL_LINK_OFF is not optional (spec 9.2, risk 17).
+ *      configd's IPConfiguration only starts DHCP on an Ethernet interface whose link it has been
+ *      told came up. Without it en1 stays at 169.254.x.x forever. The initial one is sent with the
+ *      first data session, and a second one is sent when the link goes down.
+ *
+ *   2. The QMI TLVs carry the modem's IPv4 address as a little-endian u32 whose value is already
+ *      the host-order address (10.0.0.1 arrives as 0x0A000001, spec 3.2). It is stored as such and
+ *      put on the wire by put32(), which is big-endian, exactly as HakiOS does. There is no ntohl
+ *      or htonl in this file, and there must not be: that is the silent killer the spec names.
+ *
+ * PORTER'S NOTE on one thing this file does NOT do: it never waits inside a QMI transaction. The
+ * thread that runs the sessions also drives pandora_ipa_poll() (spec 1.5's poll model, HakiOS's
+ * arrangement), so a thread asleep in a QMI wait is a data path that has stopped, and the receive
+ * endpoints' aggregation window is 500 us (spec 4.1). Every phase below is therefore a state on a
+ * 100 ms tick instead of a sleep: HakiOS's `dms_set_mode(); sleep_ms(2000); dms_set_mode()` is two
+ * ticks and a timer here, and its 6-second WDS retry is the same number of milliseconds spent the
+ * same way. Where that changes a behaviour it is said so, at the state machine.
+ */
